# @clara/web — public config. NEXT_PUBLIC_* is exposed to the browser;
# NEVER put a secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var, and never commit a real value
# here — copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill it there.

# Supabase Auth + PostgREST — cookie-session auth (lib/supabase/client.ts,
# lib/supabase/server.ts, proxy.ts) and the HUMAN governance lane later lanes
# build against (Accept-Profile/Content-Profile: clara). Tier-3 self-serve
# signup is ON for beta under 裁-57; hosted Auth must keep autoconfirm OFF and
# use the exact `{{ .RedirectTo }}?token_hash={{ .TokenHash }}&type=email` template
# recorded in README.md. The anon/publishable key is publishable by design.
#
# TRUED 2026-08-30 BY P4-4, STILL TRUE after the 裁-57 self-serve rewrite above:
# the service_role key is NOT "never used in this app" — `app/api/invite/route.ts`
# (via lib/members/invite-mail.ts) is a SERVER-ONLY Route Handler that needs it
# to mint the Supabase half of an invite link. The invariant that actually holds
# is unchanged and is what this comment states instead: the key never reaches a
# browser, and the variable carrying it is never `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed
# (docs/plan/active/frontend-handoff-2026-08-23.md §0.4, §4.3 — "nothing
# connects to Postgres directly and no service-role key ever reaches the
# browser"). See SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY below.
#
# Named ANON_KEY to match this repo's existing apps/dashboard/.env.example
# convention. Supabase's current dashboard/docs call the same value the
# "publishable key" (new key format, `sb_publishable_...`) — either key format
# works here; createBrowserClient/createServerClient just take a string.
#
# THE VALUE'S CLASS IS ENFORCED AT BUILD TIME. `pnpm --filter @clara/web build`
# runs `scripts/check-public-key.mjs` before bundling and REFUSES to build
# unless this slot holds an `sb_publishable_...` key or a legacy JWT whose
# decoded role is positively `anon`. A secret key, a `service_role` JWT, a
# malformed value or an empty slot all stop the build — `next build` inlines
# whatever is here into every browser bundle, and the variable's NAME proves
# nothing about its value (security review 2026-08-27, finding 7).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PUBLIC ORIGIN ALLOWLIST — SERVER ONLY. Comma-separated, exact origins
# (scheme + host + non-default port), e.g.
#   CLARA_PUBLIC_ORIGINS=https://app.clarabook.com,https://clarabook.com
#
# WHY IT EXISTS (Codex round 2, N3). `lib/same-origin.ts` proves a request came
# from this app's own origin by comparing the `Origin` header against a host the
# browser addressed. Behind a proxy the request URL's authority is rewritten, so
# the wall used to accept `X-Forwarded-Host` as a second source of truth — but
# that header is written by whoever spoke to us. An attacker sending BOTH
# `Origin: https://attacker.example` and `X-Forwarded-Host: attacker.example`
# satisfied the match against their own input, and the invite courier then mailed
# a link carrying BOTH bearer factors under the attacker's origin. The same wall
# is what P4-3's `/auth/confirm` POST proves itself against (`lib/supabase/proxy.ts`).
#
# What a deployment's public origins are is a fact about the DEPLOYMENT, so it is
# configured here rather than inferred from a request. `X-Forwarded-Host` is no
# longer consulted at all.
#
# UNSET IS FAIL-CLOSED, NOT PERMISSIVE: the wall falls back to the `Host` header
# and the request URL only. A local dev server works unset; a deployment behind a
# proxy that rewrites the authority will REFUSE its own same-origin POSTs until
# this is set — visibly, rather than by trusting the hop.
CLARA_PUBLIC_ORIGINS=

# Plain-HTTP loopback origins (`http://localhost`, `http://127.0.0.1`) are accepted
# ONLY when NODE_ENV is exactly `development`, or when the explicit flag below is
# exactly `1`. An absent, test, staging or malformed mode is fail-closed: otherwise
# an invite can be mailed with both bearer factors pointing at whatever is listening
# on the recipient's own machine. Next sets NODE_ENV for `next dev`/build/start.
#
# The override is for an explicitly controlled local/test harness that cannot use
# NODE_ENV=development. Never set it on a deployed instance; values other than `1`
# do nothing.
CLARA_ALLOW_INSECURE_LOOPBACK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INVITE MAIL COURIER (P4-4) — SERVER ONLY, all three. `app/api/invite/route.ts`
# reads them through lib/members/invite-mail.ts's `inviteMailCapability`, which
# is FAIL-CLOSED: with any one of them unset the courier answers 503
# `mail_not_configured` and CREATES NOTHING. That ordering is deliberate —
# `clara.invite_member` hands its plaintext token to its caller exactly once
# (`0147:418-423`), so an invite minted when no mail can go out is permanently
# unusable AND blocks that email for seven days behind CLR10 'an invite is
# already pending for this email'. NONE of these may ever gain a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix: `next build` inlines those into every browser bundle.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Supabase service-role key, used for EXACTLY ONE THING: `auth.admin.generateLink`
# ({type: "invite"}), which mints the `hashed_token` the invite link's path
# segment carries and which `verifyOtp` consumes on the accept page. It sends no
# email itself — the courier composes and sends the mail, because the 2026-08-30
# ruling puts Clara's OWN invite token in the link as `?ct=…` and no Supabase
# email template can carry it without persisting the plaintext in
# `auth.users.user_metadata` (see lib/members/invite-mail.ts's header).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Resend, the mail provider named by the ClaraBook handoff
# (docs/plan/active/frontend-handoff-2026-08-23.md:33). Called over its plain
# HTTPS API, so no npm dependency and nothing that fails on Cloudflare Workers.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From header on the invitation, e.g. `Clara <invites@yourdomain>`. Resend
# refuses a sender on an unverified domain, which surfaces to the admin as
# `mail_failed` with a CORRELATION ID — never the provider's own message. The
# courier stopped relaying upstream text entirely (independent review of #455,
# MEDIUM-3): Resend is handed the full secret invite URL, so its error strings
# have been in the same process as both bearer factors. The real, classified
# failure is on the SERVER LOG under that id, so a misconfigured sender is
# diagnosed from the log line rather than from the banner.
INVITE_MAIL_FROM=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARA_RUNTIME_URL IS GONE, DELIBERATELY, AND MUST NOT COME BACK.
# It used to sit here as "the Clara runtime, chat/SSE only" — the one lane still
# calling the runtime cross-origin from the browser. The FS-10 cutover prep
# measured that BOTH of its states were dead on a deployed origin: unset, the
# browser asked apps/web's own origin for /api/chat/* and /api/tasks/*, which
# this app has no Route Handler for (404); set, it made a cross-origin call to a
# runtime whose CORS middleware is mounted on /api/intake only, so the browser
# blocked it. The chat and task-stream lanes now ride the same-origin proxy like
# every other runtime caller (lib/clara/api.ts, lib/clara/stream.ts), and there
# is no dev-only override: an override is how the broken shape would return.

# The Clara runtime, SERVER-SIDE ONLY — app/api/runtime/[...path]/route.ts's
# same-origin proxy. It carries EVERY runtime leg this app calls: document
# intake (begin/bytes/finalize), the document-bytes evidence viewer, the durable
# interview runner, and — since the FS-10 pre-cutover fix — chat sessions,
# turns and the task SSE stream. NEVER NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed: this value must
# stay server-only and be read at REQUEST time, not baked into the browser
# bundle at build time (independent review 2026-08-27, F1/F2). Unset → the proxy
# answers a typed 503 (`runtime_not_configured`), never a silent localhost
# fallback — which now means chat is honestly unavailable rather than quietly
# addressing nothing.
CLARA_RUNTIME_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FS-4 C-6 — THE CHECKOUT GATE'S FOUR NAMES, all SERVER-ONLY. They were live in
# code and absent from this file, so this file positively DISAGREED with the
# Wave-G setup checklist (docs/ops/wave-g-setup-checklist.md, "FS-4 C-6"), which
# is the authority for the deploy-time list. Values empty here, as everywhere
# else in this file; the two shared ones move env-to-env and are never printed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The rate wall's pepper — read by lib/rate-wall-courier.ts. It must be the
# IDENTICAL value the runtime holds: both limbs (the confirm-attempt wall and
# the checkout rate wall) key on sha256(pepper ‖ value), so two different
# peppers split one wall into two that never see each other's counts. Absent ⇒
# POST /checkout refuses, fail-closed by design.
CLARA_RATE_WALL_PEPPER=

# The header carrying the client's address, and the line most likely to be got
# wrong because the SAME NAME takes a DIFFERENT correct value on each app:
#   on apps/web  → CF-Connecting-IP     (the header this app's own Cloudflare
#                                        edge sets — never X-Forwarded-For, which
#                                        any client can send)
#   on the runtime → X-Clara-Client-IP  (apps/web sits between the browser and
#                                        the runtime, so it forwards the address
#                                        ITS edge saw under that fixed name —
#                                        AUTH_WALL_CLIENT_IP_HEADER, a constant
#                                        in lib/rate-wall-courier.ts, not a
#                                        fifth variable)
# Set the runtime to any other name and its confirm endpoint answers 503 for
# every applicant with nothing in either app's configuration looking wrong.
CLARA_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# The bearer that proves a call to the runtime's confirm endpoint came from
# apps/web's server and not the open internet — that endpoint has no user
# session to check, because the caller is confirming in order to get one. Must
# be the IDENTICAL value the runtime holds; mismatched ⇒ 401 on every
# confirmation.
CLARA_AUTH_WALL_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Stripe. Read by lib/checkout/stripe-session.ts alone, server-side, and never
# bundled. TEST/sandbox mode for the whole beta (裁-81/87/126/148) — a live key
# does not belong here or on the beta deployment. NEVER NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The Stripe mode this deployment DECLARES, and the gate that makes the line
# above enforceable rather than merely stated. lib/checkout/stripe-session.ts
# reads it on every checkout and refuses BEFORE calling Stripe when the key's
# class (sk_live_/rk_live_ vs sk_test_/rk_test_) disagrees with it — in either
# direction. Vocabulary: 1/true/live or 0/false/test; anything else, including
# UNSET, is "unconfigured" and refuses every checkout. Fail-closed on purpose:
# a deployment that has not said which Stripe it is talking to must not be
# allowed to guess.
#
# NOT A SECRET, so on the deployed Worker its home is wrangler.jsonc's `vars`
# block (already set to `test`), not `wrangler secret put`. This entry is for
# local development. Same name, same vocabulary as the RUNTIME's own gate
# (packages/runtime/lib/stripe-livemode.mjs), which refuses a webhook event
# whose livemode disagrees — one deployment fact, read by both arms.
CLARA_STRIPE_LIVEMODE=test
